<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<h3>Do I need sunglasses if it&#8217;s cloudy?</h3>
<p>Yes. UV rays pass through cloud cover. A grey sky in July still carries enough UV to cause damage, especially near water, where it reflects off the surface regardless of how bright the sun looks.</p>
<h3>Are expensive sunglasses better for UV protection?</h3>
<p>Price has nothing to do with it. A $20 pair with a UV400 label protects your eyes. A $300 pair without it doesn&#8217;t. The label is the only thing that tells you what the lens is actually doing.</p>
<h3>Can I wear my contacts to the beach?</h3>
<p>You can wear them to the beach, but take them out before you go in the water. Ocean water carries bacteria that can get trapped between the lens and your eye. Always bring a case and solution, and wear UV400 sunglasses over your contacts the whole time you&#8217;re outside.</p>
<h3>Do my UV-blocking contacts replace sunglasses?</h3>
<p>No. UV-blocking contacts cover only your cornea. The white of your eye, your eyelids, and the skin around your eyes are still fully exposed. Sunglasses are still necessary.</p>
<h3>Is polarization the same as UV protection?</h3>
<p>No. Polarization cuts glare from reflective surfaces like water, which makes it useful at the beach. But it does not block UV rays. A polarized lens still needs a UV400 rating to protect your eye health.</p>
<h3>Can I get prescription lenses in sunglasses?</h3>
<p>Yes, and it&#8217;s worth doing if you wear glasses. Prescription sunglasses with UV400 lenses give you the same clarity you have indoors without layering anything or squinting through non-prescription lenses. Bring your prescription in, and we&#8217;ll sort you out.</p>
